# melodiespark-cli

Python frontend for the Melodiespark content catalog and template CLI.

```
pip install melodiespark-cli
pipx install melodiespark-cli
```

The same catalog, the same classification rules, the same templates, and the
same policy as the TypeScript CLI (`@melodiespark/cli`). One catalog, two
frontends.

## Why this package exists

Two frontends, one contract. The TypeScript CLI builds the catalog; this one
reads it. Neither re-implements the extraction rules, because two independent
implementations of a security policy drift — and they drift permissively,
silently.

A parity test in the Node package runs both frontends against one catalog and
asserts they agree on summary counts, per-category counts, per-query counts,
template sets, and the set of failing `verify` checks. If Python is unavailable,
that test reports `SKIPPED` rather than passing.

**Known limitation, accepted:** building the catalog is Node-only. Run
`melodie catalog --build` once from a checkout. A wheel ships the catalog as
package data, so this only matters when the catalog is stale.

## Commands

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `melodiespark-cli catalog` | Summarize the catalog. |
| `melodiespark-cli inspect <path…>` | Query catalog metadata. Never prints file contents. |
| `melodiespark-cli extract <path…>` | Copy content. **Deny by default.** Every decision is audited. |
| `melodiespark-cli template [name]` | List and describe templates. |
| `melodiespark-cli init <dir> -t <name>` | Scaffold a project. |
| `melodiespark-cli verify` | Catalog integrity plus a secret-leak scan. `--deep` re-hashes the source. |
| `melodiespark-cli status` | Capability status, with evidence. |

Every command accepts `--json`.

## The one rule

> **The catalog says a file exists. It does not expose its contents.**

## Deny by default

```bash
melodiespark-cli inspect --category SOURCE          # see what exists
melodiespark-cli extract "src/index.py" --dry-run  # decide, write nothing
melodiespark-cli extract "src/index.py" --out ./SOURCE
```

Two independent layers:

1. **Path and filename patterns.** A copy of a known secret in a directory
   nobody enumerated is still denied.
2. **Content scan.** Before content is copied, it is scanned for high-signal
   credential patterns. A live key pasted into an ordinary `.md` file is caught
   even though the path looks harmless.

`SECRET` is **permanently** denied. `--i-have-authorization` does not unlock it.

Decisions append to `extract-audit.jsonl` next to the destination.

## Command name collision — read this before installing

This package declares **both** console scripts `melodiespark-cli` and
`melodiespark`. The existing `melodiespark-py` package also declares
`melodiespark`.

Installing both into one environment makes the second install overwrite the
first one's shim. They are different programs. This is unresolved and is a
product decision, not a technical one — see `DECISIONS.md` D9.

**Until it is resolved, do not install `melodiespark` and `melodiespark-cli` into
the same environment.** `melodiespark-cli` is unambiguous and safe.

## Development

```bash
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
python scripts/sync_data.py                          # copy catalog + rules + templates into the package
python -m unittest discover -s tests -v              # 18 policy + dispatch tests
python -m build
python -m twine check dist/*
```

`sync_data.py` must run before `python -m build`. It copies the shared catalog,
the classification rules, and the templates into
`src/melodiespark_cli/data/` so the wheel is self-contained and does not need
the monorepo present at runtime.

## Status

Packaging is validated locally: wheel and sdist build, `twine check` passes, the
wheel installs into a clean virtualenv, and `pipx` installs it. See
`BUILD_REPORT.md`.

**Nothing has been published to PyPI.** No registry credentials were used or
requested. Local build success is not publication.

The presence of this package, a template, or a scaffold is not evidence that any
described capability is built, tested, secure, or operational.
